## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** patterns that break on .NET 8:

| Line | API | Problem on .NET 8 |
|------|-----|--------------------|
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `Thread.Interrupt` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9); throws `ThreadInterruptedException` which `WaitOne` doesn't reliably surface |
| `_thread.Abort()` | `Thread.Abort` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Blocking call | Can't be interrupted by `CancellationToken` without `WaitAny` |

Additionally, there's a **race condition bug** in the original: multiple `Enqueue` calls can `Set` the event, but `Reset` + single `Dequeue` loses items.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny wakes on EITHER a new event OR cancellation
            WaitHandle.WaitAny([_newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle]);

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            // Drain all queued items (fixes original race condition)
            List<string> items;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                items = new List<string>(_queue);
                _queue.Clear();
            }

            foreach (var item in items)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for graceful exit
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation only
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Interrupt is obsolete; CTS wakes `WaitAny` via `token.WaitHandle` |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Removed | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`; cooperative loop exits naturally |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny([event, token])` | Allows cancellation to wake the blocked thread |
| Single `Dequeue` after `Reset` | Drain all items | Fixes lost-event race when multiple `Enqueue` calls happen before `WaitOne` returns |
| No `IDisposable` | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` and `ManualResetEventSlim` must be disposed |

## Pitfalls to Watch

1. **`WaitOne` can't be cancelled alone** — You *must* use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`. Without this, `Stop()` hangs because nothing wakes the blocked thread.

2. **Don't assume cancellation is instant** — The loop only exits at the `WaitAny` checkpoint. If `Handle(item)` runs for 30 seconds, `Stop()` waits up to 30s + 5s join. Pass the token into `Handle` if it does long work.

3. **Dispose the `CancellationTokenSource`** — Leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. The `IDisposable` pattern ensures cleanup.

4. **No fallback for uncooperative code** — If `Handle()` truly hangs and can't be modified, the only .NET 8 option is to move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()`. There is no in-process equivalent of `Thread.Abort`.
